slice              cases   passed   rate
python/easy          40      38     95.0%
typescript/hard      40      33     82.5%
go/hard              20      17     85.0%

The 95% Wilson interval for k passes out of n is (p + z^2/2n +/- z * sqrt(p(1-p)/n + z^2/4n^2)) / (1 + z^2/n), with p = k/n and z = 1.96.

Statement: "A true pass rate of 70% on go/hard is consistent with the 17 out of 20 measured here at 95% confidence."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. For 17 out of 20 the 95% Wilson interval is about 64% to 95%, and 70% lies inside it. With only 20 cases, a measured 85% cannot rule out a slice that is really much weaker; you would need far more go/hard cases (or a much larger difference) before treating that slice as good. The misconception is reading a rate from a small slice as if it were exact: the number of cases decides how much the rate can move, which is why a report should always show n and an interval next to every slice rate.', 1, 5
