$ npm run eval -- --prompt grader-v8 --set evals/grader/v3
verdict matches label: 324 / 400 (81.0%)
$ npm run eval -- --prompt grader-v9 --set evals/grader/v3
verdict matches label: 372 / 400 (93.0%)
$ node evals/check/leakage.js --prompt grader-v9 --set evals/grader/v3
few-shot example ex-1 matches eval case grader-v3-0142 (typescript/hard)
few-shot example ex-2 matches eval case grader-v3-0307 (go/medium)
few-shot example ex-3 matches eval case grader-v3-0388 (adversarial)
3 leaks found

Statement: "Because both runs used the same eval set version, the 12-point gain is a fair estimate of how much better grader-v9 will grade production submissions."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Using the same set version is necessary for a fair comparison but not sufficient: the leakage check shows that grader-v9''s prompt contains three of the eval cases, so the model sees those inputs (and the expected judgement) at grading time. On top of that, the examples were chosen as the best of 14 tries scored on this very set, so the prompt is fitted to these 400 cases: some of the gain is selection on noise and on the set''s quirks, and it will shrink on new submissions. Pick few-shot examples from outside the eval set, tune on a dev split, and measure the final candidate once on a test split it has never seen before trusting any gain. The misconception is that a fixed eval set is automatically an unbiased judge of every prompt run against it.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_111') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
