WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_121'), 'Recorded output of one case (the model call timed out):

{"caseId":"grader-v3-0311","output":null,"error":"timeout after 30000 ms"}

Assertions of the case:
[{ "type": "not_contains", "value": "def solve(" }, { "type": "max_length", "value": 600 }]

Runner code:
const text = recorded.output ?? "";
const failed = assertions.filter((a) => !check(a, text));

Report line:
PASS grader-v3-0311 (not_contains, max_length)

Statement: "The PASS shows that for this submission the grader returned feedback under 600 characters that does not reveal the reference solution."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The model returned nothing: the call timed out and the output is null. The runner replaced null with an empty string, and an empty string trivially contains no forbidden text and is shorter than any limit, so both assertions passed on an answer that does not exist. A failed call must fail every assertion (and be counted, so a spike of timeouts shows up in the report). The misconception is that "no assertion failed" means "the output is good"; it only means none of the checks could find a problem in what they were given.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_121') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
