    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_121'), 'Eval report (every case is valid; weight is per case, the overall score is the sum of the weights of passing cases divided by the sum of the weights of all cases):

slice             weight   cases   passed
python/easy          1       30      29
typescript/hard      1       20      16
adversarial          3       10       5

unweighted pass rate: 50 / 60 = 83.3%

Statement: "The overall weighted score is 0.75, lower than the unweighted 83.3%, because each adversarial case counts three times and that slice passes only half of its cases."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The weighted score is (29 x 1 + 16 x 1 + 5 x 3) / (30 x 1 + 20 x 1 + 10 x 3) = 60 / 80 = 0.75. The adversarial slice is 10 of 60 cases (17%) but 30 of 80 weight units (37.5%), so its 50% pass rate pulls the score down much more than in the unweighted rate. That is the point of the weights: a grader that gives full marks to a submission asking for them is a worse failure than a style nit on an easy case. The misconception is that weighting only re-scales the number; it changes which failures dominate it.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_121') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
