    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_231'), 'The log shipper redacts national ID numbers (12 digits) with:

line = line.replace(/\b\d{12}\b/g, "[NATIONAL_ID]");

Input line:
2026-10-27T09:15:42Z appeal sub-202610270915 learner_cccd=079203001234 course=course_204512345678

Statement: "On this line the regex redacts the learner''s national ID and nothing else."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. \b is a boundary between a word character (letters, digits, _) and a non-word character. "-" is not a word character, so there is a boundary between "sub-" and "202610270915", and the submission id is redacted too: the line becomes "appeal sub-[NATIONAL_ID] learner_cccd=[NATIONAL_ID]". The course id survives only because "_" is a word character, so there is no boundary between "course_" and the digits. The two ids are now indistinguishable from the learner''s ID, and the submission can no longer be found from the log. The misconception is that \b means "a whole token"; state the characters that may not touch the match (for example letters, digits, "_" and "-") with lookbehind and lookahead instead, and test the rule on real ids from your logs.', 1, 5
