prompt: "Grade the submission of [NAME_1] ([EMAIL_1]). Rubric: ... Submission: ... # reviewed with [NAME_2]"
output: "Great work, [NAME_1]! A copy of this feedback was sent to [EMAIL_1]."
counts: { EMAIL: 2, NAME: 3, PHONE: 0, NATIONAL_ID: 0 }

Statement: "From this redacted span alone, an engineer can tell that the model repeated the learner''s email address in its output."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Consistent pseudonyms give the same value the same placeholder within one document, so [EMAIL_1] in the output is the same address as [EMAIL_1] in the prompt, and the counts confirm two email occurrences. The engineer sees that the model echoed personal data into learner-facing text (a bug worth a guardrail) without seeing the address itself. A redaction that replaced everything with a bare "[REDACTED]" would hide exactly this. The misconception is that redaction must destroy all structure; it only has to remove the values. Note that the numbering restarts in every document, so [EMAIL_1] is not a user id that can be joined across traces - which is the point.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_231') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
