WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_321'), 'Trace of one learning-assistant call, manifest attribute (gen_ai.prompt.manifest):
[{"name":"submission","boundary":"t4k9w2","chars":1840,"sentChars":1840,"truncated":false,"escaped":6,"normalised":false},
 {"name":"question","boundary":"h7c1m5","chars":212,"sentChars":212,"truncated":false,"escaped":0,"normalised":false},
 {"name":"notes","boundary":"x2p8d6","chars":61234,"sentChars":4000,"truncated":true,"escaped":0,"normalised":true}]

A support ticket claims the assistant followed an instruction that appears at character 60,100 of the "notes" input.

Statement: "The manifest alone shows that this instruction was never sent to the model inside the notes block: only the first 4,000 of its 61,234 characters were included."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. sentChars 4000 with truncated true means the builder kept the first 4,000 characters (after normalising and escaping) and replaced the rest with a truncation line; character 60,100 lies far beyond that. This is what the manifest is for: it records what was included without storing the raw untrusted text in the trace, so an engineer can answer "did the model see this?" from the trace alone. It does not prove the model did not follow some other instruction, and normalised true is worth a look too: it means the note contained compatibility characters such as full-width forms. The misconception is that you need the full prompt in the logs to investigate an injection report.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_lle_321') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
